WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Hello, and welcome to Lecture 29, Multicollinearity and Ridge Regression.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:17.000
So we learned ridge regression last lecture, so here we're going to see where it's one of its best use cases.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.000
So what is collinearity and what is orthogonality?

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:31.000
So let's look at, we call it the geometry of the predictor. So if I have just two random predictors, say X and Z here, X and Z.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:39.000
If a predictor is orthogonal, when the columns are orthogonal, one predictor contains no information about the other.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.000
So this almost looks like some sort of residual plot you learned in year one, and so there's no real relationship between the two.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Collinear, when predictors move together, they contain overlapping information.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.000
And what do we do with that? That's what we're going to discuss today. What do you do when you have collinearity?

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:01.000
Oftentimes you have a little bit even, because just randomness can induce some collinearity.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:10.000
So, yeah, it's kind of a, what do we, what do we do with it?

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.000
And so what does it do to our model? So let's just say we have this model.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:23.000
We've got an intercept. We've got two terms, A and B, and we're saying this is what we've got.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:32.000
So we define this relationship. How can we get rid of that relationship?

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Well, we can actually go and take the values of B and shuffle the order of them and then calculate a new Y based on that shuffled B.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:50.000
And if I do that, I end up with, there is still some relationship, but mostly it's almost random now.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:59.000
And so if I have shuffled values, what's actually happening in my model?

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:08.000
So correlated values, we've got a linear model, Y is AB, uncorrelated Y rand, because I have a B rand, and that makes a Y rand.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.000
So basically it is still A plus B. This is data I generated.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Let's stick it together and make it look nice.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:20.000
So intercept 48 and 2, intercept 53 and 3, so that's basically the same.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Our relationships between correlated predictors A and B, 4.76 and 5.29 versus 4.99 and 4.83.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.000
These are both supposed to be 5, right?

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:37.000
So it didn't really change much, correlated versus uncorrelated.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:42.000
What did happen was they uncorrelated when the standard errors dropped.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:52.000
So we are able to more precisely estimate the parameter when there's less correlation, which is kind of the theme.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:58.000
So what happens if you have what's called exact dependence?

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:06.000
So this is, if you haven't taken linear algebra, you can just think more generally.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:14.000
If you have a relationship and it is Z equals alpha 0 plus alpha 1 X,

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:19.000
then Z contains no interpretation that's not already determined by the intercept and X.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:23.000
So there are infinitely many coefficients that give the exact same fitted values.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:29.000
So basically if I do any sort of linear model, remember these are normally betas, but here we have alphas,

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:35.000
any sort of linear model to Z, so the intercept doesn't affect the correlation, right?

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:40.000
It just shifts it up and down, which you saw that three or four lectures back.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:43.000
There's not new information.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.000
So what does this look like?

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.000
Let's take some data, make it 1 to 8.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.000
Z is equal to 2 plus 3XY.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:57.000
Y is equal to blah, blah, blah, and then we'll fit X equals Y plus Z.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:10.000
And when we fit it, since X and Z are correlated,

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:17.000
we end up being unable to estimate them both in the same model.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:19.000
You just get N/As.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:27.000
This is kind of a huge sign if you ever see N/As in your model that there might be some collinearity involved.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.000
The problem is this is really extreme.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.000
This is really extreme.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:40.000
If there's even a little bit of randomness in this, you would still get an estimate out.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:47.000
So this is kind of an issue when we're building models.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:50.000
We say the design is rank deficient.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:55.000
This has to do with inverting the matrix, how we fit the hat matrix.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:00.000
We can't do it with linearly independent columns, so one column can't make another.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.000
As I was just saying, near-dependence is less obvious, and it's going to be a lot trickier.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:07.000
So we're going to look at that next.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:13.000
Using real data, we're going to model bill using household income, number of persons, and floor area.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.000
These things are probably related.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:21.000
Income and floor area are probably related.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:25.000
Persons and floor area are probably related.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:28.000
But let's look.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:31.000
Oh no, my graphic didn't show up.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:34.000
There will be a graphic online, but the graphic didn't show up.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:42.000
So basically, area and income are strongly correlated, and persons overlap much less.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:46.000
So the image isn't up here, but it will be on the notes.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:50.000
So what does this look like for the calculations?

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:54.000
Bill is area, persons, income, and then we calculate it.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.000
So intercept, minus 350, okay.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:04.000
Area, 0.28 times area, 55 persons, 0.07 times income, 0.8514.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:11.000
Yeah, if I gave you this data and you plug this in, residuals are roughly symmetric there.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:17.000
It looks like area is not significant, and income is not significant, and neither is persons.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:22.000
But it's still explaining a lot for having non-significant terms, right?

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:26.000
The model overall is significant, but the individual terms are not.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:31.000
So what should make us suspicious about this?

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:36.000
The overall regression can look useful in the individual terms of large standard errors.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:38.000
That's a really big standard error.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.000
So, yeah, 29 on 55 is big.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:44.000
0.13 on 7, that's twice as big.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:47.000
0.22 and 0.28, that's like, you know, like 90%.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:53.000
So those are really large, so that's kind of what I have to worry about.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:58.000
Coefficient signs or magnitudes can change sharply when correlated predictors enter together.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:02.000
So if we took in and out terms, we could potentially see this.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:06.000
And then small changes to the formula can reduce to large changes to the conditional coefficients.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:12.000
You enter in a new term, and things start changing a lot.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:14.000
So we will take a look at this.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:18.000
Let's fit area and income separately and then together.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:25.000
So area only model, bill and area, income only, bill and income, area, income, bill, area, and income.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:27.000
And so let's stick it all together and make it look nice.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:30.000
So let's look at the area term.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.000
And I get 0.438.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:35.000
But area and income together, the area term is 0.6.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:36.000
So it's 80% bigger.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:39.000
Standard area goes from 0.03 to 0.12.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:43.000
And the P value, they're still both significant.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:46.000
So that's quite a big shift, and that's a big shift in standard area.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:50.000
Income, ooh, so 0.29 to negative.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.000
Whoa, it's shifted directions.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:55.000
That's also pretty crazy.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:00.000
Standard area has also gone up four times, but it's still significant -- not significant, 0.1.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:05.000
So income lost its significance.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:07.000
So by themselves, they're significant.

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:13.000
But when they enter together, the model is asking a conditional question.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:17.000
What is the association with income among houses of the same area?

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:23.000
Because they move together so strongly, area and income, the data contain little independent information.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:26.000
This should make intuitive sense.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:28.000
So this is what collinear means.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:31.000
These are two things that are really related to each other.

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:35.000
There's not new information, so adding them to the model is not helpful.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:42.000
So if anyone asks you what type of -- which type of data is best to enter in the model,

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:48.000
you'd say data that's not correlated with any current variable has the highest chance of adding new information to a model,

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:51.000
because it's not capturing anything that you've already captured.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:55.000
It could be something else that you've missed previously.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:58.000
So let's look at the person's coefficient.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:03.000
Let's do bill is area in persons and bill is income in persons.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:07.000
So persons, area in persons, estimates 42 to 85.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:09.000
I mean it changes.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:13.000
It's significant in both persons.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:16.000
The estimate changes, but the standard is about the same.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:23.000
So we see the coefficient of persons has changed, conditional on what else it is.

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:26.000
This isn't just numerical nuisance.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:32.000
With correlated predictors, the actual interpretation of the coefficient depends on what else is held fixed, right?

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:38.000
So we see if this was my model, we'd say persons adds this much to the bill.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:40.000
And for each additional person, that's $40.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:44.000
But if I do income, I see each additional person adds $80.

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:45.000
What?

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:49.000
And so the actual interpretation is changing when you stick in this correlated variable.

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:53.000
So we need to figure out something to do with them.

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:55.000
So how do we detect them?

00:09:55.000 --> 00:10:01.000
We'll do that first, and then we'll figure out how we can kind of enumerate the issues.

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:04.000
So pairwise plots are useful, but we really want a diagnostic.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:09.000
So how well can each predictor be explained by all other predictors together?

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:15.000
So this is us essentially regressing one predictor versus every other predictor.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:21.000
So the i-th predictor versus everything but that predictor, all the way up to all the predictors.

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:23.000
And we call these r sub squares of i.

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:30.000
If it's close to 1, most of the variation in xi is already explained by the other predictors.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:36.000
Very little independent information remains for estimating a separate conditional coefficient.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:38.000
That should make sense.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:44.000
So if I'm able to take a bunch of other predictors and essentially recreate a predictor,

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:46.000
then there's not much new information.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:49.000
But we usually don't look at it as just the r square.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:52.000
We look at it as something called the variance inflation factor,

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:55.000
and that's just 1 over 1 minus that r square.

00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:59.000
And so if r square is 0, VIF is 1.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:01.000
There's no indication of overlap.

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:08.000
As r square approaches 1, the variance inflation increases rapidly for a numerical predictor.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:10.000
Essentially, this number goes to the moon.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:12.000
It gets really big.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:15.000
So the variance of these beta i's, we get our variance estimate,

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:19.000
n minus 1 over variance times 1 over r squared.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:24.000
The final factor tells us how much variance of that coefficient

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:27.000
has been enlarged by a linear overlap with other predictors,

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:33.000
holding the residual variance and spread of xi fixed.

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:38.000
So the VIF is essentially able--

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:39.000
we'll look at it here on the next slide--

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:44.000
but essentially it's able to show us how--

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:48.000
it's apples-to-apples comparisons of all the variables, essentially,

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:49.000
is the way to think about it.

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:51.000
Is there a way I can look at all the variables,

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:54.000
adjusting for all the other variables but that variable,

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:58.000
and then make a determination of which ones are related to each other.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:00.000
To calculate it, we use the CAR package.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:01.000
It's really funny.

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:02.000
It's called CAR.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:03.000
I always thought that was funny.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:04.000
And you just call it VIF.

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:06.000
And we can give it a model.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:08.000
But this is the baseline electric model.

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:09.000
And it says, oh, look.

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:11.000
My VIF value for area is 44.

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:12.000
Persons is 3.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:13.000
Income is 39.

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:21.000
So the large values for area and income said that these two are highly related.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:24.000
And the other cool thing about VIFs is they almost always come in pairs,

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.000
unless you have three variables that are related.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:32.000
The variables that are collinear with each other are going to be related--

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:37.000
are going to have roughly the same size inflation factors.

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:39.000
So the other thing to think about that is,

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:44.000
if I have two sets of correlated coefficients--

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:47.000
like one set could be in the 40s, one set could be in the 100s,

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:50.000
whatever value it ends up being--

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:52.000
then you could say these two are related and these two are related.

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:55.000
So if I show you a VIF for a bunch of variables,

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:58.000
you should be able to look at it and go, oh,

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:03.000
this one and this one are the ones likely to be causing it.

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:07.000
We do need to remember that VIF is not a diagnostic

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:11.000
and it's not an instruction to delete a variable.

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:13.000
Again, VIF is not a diagnostic

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:15.000
and it's not an instruction to delete a variable.

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:17.000
So VIF can be there.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:19.000
There can be reasons why you need to keep both of them.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:24.000
You might think of predicting university success

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:26.000
and the admissions office looks at it

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:29.000
and there's some sort of NCEA score they look at

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:31.000
and they also look at your GPA.

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:34.000
Those two might be highly related to each other.

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:38.000
But they need them both because they do give slightly different information.

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:42.000
So there can be a defensible reason why they keep them both in the model.

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:44.000
This just tells you you need to look out for it

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:48.000
and maybe there's something you need to do with the modeling to account for it.

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:52.000
This is a really-- I always like this example.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:58.000
It's SAP data relates sales to previous advertising,

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:02.000
current and previous promotion spending, and sales expense.

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:04.000
And so this data was given to an analyst

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:07.000
and they didn't know that four of the predictors

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:09.000
were constrained by a fixed budget.

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:12.000
So current advertising and past advertising,

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:16.000
current promotion spending and past promotion spending

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:19.000
are roughly equal to five of whatever units.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:22.000
It could be millions, it could be thousands, it doesn't matter.

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:26.000
So when this constraint is there, these things are related to each other.

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:31.000
But you see this is not as simple a relationship as we've seen before.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:33.000
This is a more complicated relationship.

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:38.000
So the relationships that cause these correlations can be quite complex.

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:42.000
So if we model this out,

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:47.000
present and previous advertising, sales expense, promotion spending--

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:49.000
we just do a regular model and we're looking at it and we're like,

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:51.000
Okay, what did we get here?

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:57.000
We get overall it fits really great, 0.91, adjusted our square,

00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:59.000
whole model looks good.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:03.000
P1 and A1, not significant.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:06.000
Sales expenditure, that looks good, that's highly significant.

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:09.000
And maybe I'm going to get a promotion spending,

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:14.000
so I could just say these two and A and P.

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:18.000
So we're trying to estimate separate advertising and promotion coefficients

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:21.000
from predictors that are close to linearly constant.

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:25.000
So if we look at the VIF of this model, we end up with--

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:33.000
oh, wow, look at this, we got A, P, standard error is low, A1, P1.

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:35.000
Holy moly.

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:38.000
Yeah, these are all really large.

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:40.000
We want these values.

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:42.000
Some people say around 2.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:44.000
I'm not going to tell you exactly where it is,

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:46.000
but you want them as low as possible.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:53.000
A few is fine, 20s and 30s is bad, 10s, maybe take a look at it.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:55.000
It's not giving you good information.

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:56.000
So what can we do?

00:15:56.000 --> 00:15:58.000
We can drop a term and see if that fixes it.

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:05.000
So we can do PSEA1P1 or APSEP1.

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:13.000
So we're dropping A1 here and we're dropping A here.

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:15.000
Let's look at what we get.

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:18.000
So this is just tidying it up so we can look at it.

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:23.000
If we drop A, P becomes 3.7.

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:26.000
P and P dropping A1 becomes 4.

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:28.000
Okay, those are roughly similar.

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:30.000
Yep, standard error.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:34.000
SE22 and SE22, yep.

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:38.000
A1, P1, minus 7, minus 0.9.

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:41.000
A1 we dropped, so P1.

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:43.000
Ooh, P1, you see it's jumping.

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:45.000
When it jumps signs, that's usually a sign.

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:51.000
Although it does tend to happen the closer they are to zero already,

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:53.000
if that makes sense.

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:54.000
So it changes.

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:59.000
This relationship is essentially changing depending upon which member is removed,

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:02.000
and that's what happens when you have a linear dependency.

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:08.000
So the other question we can ask is--so this is what the VIF is based upon.

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:11.000
Can we reconstruct this A term advertising?

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:13.000
So given that we know this is a linear relationship,

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:15.000
can we reconstruct R-squared of A?

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:16.000
And so we do that.

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:22.000
We just say A is approximately P plus SE plus A1 plus P1, yep.

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:25.000
And we end up with this multiple R-squared of 0.97.

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:34.000
So, yes, we're basically able to completely reconstruct A using a linear set of terms.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:35.000
It's amazing, right?

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:38.000
So you don't want to see this in your data, and they're all highly significant.

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:45.000
And then SE, once again, does not help because it's not really related to that term.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:49.000
So that's cool that we saw the terms can change.

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:53.000
We need to look at variance inflation factors.

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:56.000
But there's no magic bullet on this.

00:17:56.000 --> 00:18:00.000
This is where modeling becomes more art than science.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:04.000
There's things we can do, but there's not one thing I can tell you what to do,

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:07.000
and this is always what you should do.

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:12.000
So we can remove or redefine one of the overlapping predictors.

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:16.000
If you can do this, this is usually a good way to go.

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:18.000
Better measurement, less missingness.

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:20.000
And so, yeah, real data is usually missing some stuff.

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:24.000
So if one variable has more data, oftentimes it's the one to use.

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:28.000
Or do you know something about the system?

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:31.000
You can construct a combined quantity.

00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:37.000
So you could do an average, composite, a difference, a ratio.

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:45.000
So one thing you'll see a lot is BMI, height over kilogram squared, I think it is.

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:47.000
Or is it kilograms over height squared?

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:48.000
I don't remember what it is.

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:52.000
So BMI is one of these combined things that are highly related,

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:57.000
but together they give you information so you can come up with some new metric.

00:18:57.000 --> 00:19:03.000
Or you can use penalized regression, which is what we learned earlier.

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:06.000
So Ridge uses an L2 and Lasso is an L1.

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:13.000
And so Ridge is really what's cool here because by shrinking down those parameters,

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:20.000
by penalizing them, we essentially allow ourselves to estimate the parameters

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:28.000
a bit more stably in the presence of multi-correlation.

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:31.000
So it doesn't make them independent.

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:35.000
It just makes it a little bit easier to estimate.

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:38.000
So let's look at this sole bike data.

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:44.000
Other predictors, they have temperature, humidity, dew point.

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:46.000
And the strongest relation is 0.13.

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:48.000
Once again, the images are going to be in the notes.

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:49.000
They're not here.

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:52.000
That's a technical issue.

00:19:52.000 --> 00:19:54.000
So let's look at what we've got here.

00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:56.000
So we had rented bike, temperature, humidity, dew point,

00:19:56.000 --> 00:20:00.000
solar radiation, rainfall, snowfall, just to remind you of what we've got.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.000
So we look at a bunch of these significant, significant, significant, significant,

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:05.000
significant, only thing that's not is dew point.

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:07.000
So you're like, wow, okay, these are all good.

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:11.000
And you remember we did stepwise with these models.

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:17.000
So we thought we got a decent model with our stepwise doing this.

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:21.000
What we hadn't checked was are the variables related.

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:24.000
And so if I'm looking at this, I'm like, whoa,

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:30.000
it looks like temperature and dew point are going to be related to each other.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:36.000
Humidity also is, which isn't a surprise, but not near to the extent as these guys.

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:40.000
Snowfall around one, rainfall around one, solar radiation around 1.8.

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:46.000
Yeah, so it still can be useful, remember, because all models are wrong.

00:20:46.000 --> 00:20:48.000
But we need to do something.

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:54.000
Maybe we can adjust a bit differently.

00:20:54.000 --> 00:21:01.000
So let's look at a couple different intercept terms across the Lambda 1 SE

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:04.000
on the penalized regression to see kind of what happens.

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:05.000
So we've got ordinarily square.

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:07.000
So this is our baseline.

00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:10.000
We've got a ridge intercept.

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:16.000
See how it's constrained the estimate towards zero.

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:17.000
So this goes up.

00:21:17.000 --> 00:21:19.000
This one goes down.

00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:22.000
This one flips.

00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:24.000
Hmm, okay.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:26.000
But it is actually constrained.

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:28.000
Solar radiation is way down.

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:32.000
So it goes from 100 to minus 16, minus 48 to minus 46, 42 to minus 4.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:34.000
So you can see the ones that we had --

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:40.000
so some of these are just controlled a bit more than others as the ridge allows this.

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:43.000
And these estimates would be much more stable.

00:21:43.000 --> 00:21:48.000
Versus a lasso, which applies, if you remember the image,

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:52.000
differential shrinkage to those colored balls.

00:21:52.000 --> 00:21:55.000
And so this is why temperature is very similar.

00:21:55.000 --> 00:21:57.000
And this is actually pretty similar.

00:21:57.000 --> 00:21:58.000
And this guy is totally zero.

00:21:58.000 --> 00:21:59.000
Dew point is just gone.

00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:00.000
Lasso is like nope.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:03.000
And lasso is like snowfall, nope.

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:06.000
Rainfall, it's a little bit smaller.

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:09.000
And then the solar radiation, it also drops it.

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:12.000
So lasso has actually dropped two terms,

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:14.000
where ridge is never going to drop terms basically.

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:16.000
I mean it could theoretically, but usually it doesn't.

00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:20.000
And then finally, there's the other option that we gave you,

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:23.000
which is just to drop dew point.

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:27.000
So if we manually drop dew point, what happens to these other terms?

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:29.000
This is roughly the same.

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:30.000
Roughly the same.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:32.000
Roughly the same.

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:33.000
Roughly the same.

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:34.000
Roughly the same.

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:39.000
So this was just a very unstable metric.

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:40.000
And if we can get rid of it.

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:41.000
All right.

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:45.000
So if I'm doing this, I would probably drop dew point.

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:49.000
I wouldn't necessarily go in and do a ridge or a lasso unless I had a reason to.

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:52.000
So what are they relating?

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:55.000
The OLS estimates unpenalized conditional coefficients.

00:22:55.000 --> 00:22:58.000
Ridge keeps whole predictor, but regularizes unstable.

00:22:58.000 --> 00:23:00.000
Lasso can shrink to zero.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:02.000
Drop dew point changes the model itself.

00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:04.000
Yeah, because it's actually a different question.

00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:07.000
And the remaining predictors have to absorb that information.

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:15.000
There is a distinction here between prediction and coefficient interpretation.

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:20.000
So some of these models are going to predict similarly

00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:24.000
while they have very different numerical coefficients to overlap in predictors.

00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:27.000
So I want you to think about that one.

00:23:27.000 --> 00:23:31.000
So I can have different parameter estimates,

00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:35.000
but I'm still just as good at predicting.

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:36.000
Right?

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:39.000
So this is once again the whole all models are wrong.

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:43.000
Some models are useful.

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:52.000
And as we look ahead to next lecture, we have numerical and categorical.

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:53.000
We can search through things.

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:56.000
We can do penalized regressions.

00:23:56.000 --> 00:24:00.000
We now know we need to check for variance inflation factors

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:08.000
and potentially do that before we start our stepwise or penalized searches.

00:24:08.000 --> 00:24:10.000
And then in the sole data, stepwise can behave poorly.

00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:12.000
Predictors can do much the same information.

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:14.000
It has to decide which ones to keep and which ones to remove.

00:24:14.000 --> 00:24:15.000
Yeah.

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:17.000
So go back and run that sole data and kick out

00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:19.000
a few terms that are highly correlated.

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:22.000
And does it make the same decisions?

00:24:22.000 --> 00:24:27.000
And let me know how it is on the discussion board.

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:35.000
So we've always assumed that the mean response changes linearly

00:24:35.000 --> 00:24:40.000
with our numerical predictors.

00:24:40.000 --> 00:24:41.000
Right?

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:42.000
So it's a linear model.

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:43.000
So it changes linearly.

00:24:43.000 --> 00:24:44.000
Yep.

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:49.000
And we've also always assumed that every measurement has the same precision.

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:57.000
So that's basically if I measure pony A and I measure pony B,

00:24:57.000 --> 00:25:00.000
the information I get from both is equally reliable.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:05.000
What would happen if I can measure pony A much better than pony B?

00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:08.000
Should I give it more weight and say, you know,

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:10.000
I actually know this is a better measurement?

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:14.000
It could be, you know, there's some clinical studies done

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:17.000
and I have 10,000 people and you have 500.

00:25:17.000 --> 00:25:21.000
We have the same outcome.

00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:29.000
But do you give â€“ yeah, do you trust my 500 as much as your 10,000?

00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:31.000
And so we're going to look at that next lecture.

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:34.000
The lecture after that is splines.

00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:38.000
And that's where the fitted mean itself can bend.

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:39.000
And so this is so cool.

00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:44.000
So now we can â€“ we're going to kind of do baby steps into time series.

00:25:44.000 --> 00:25:48.000
And so we're going â€“ these are all still technically linear models.

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:50.000
And I'll show you why as we get there.

00:25:50.000 --> 00:25:54.000
But, yeah, it allows us to model much more complex shapes.

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:55.860
And so--
